title: Relinking orphans
tags: [[Orphans]]
kind: guide
summary: mf orphan relink puts an orphan's metadata back onto a tracked file with the same content — the orphan's uuid survives, the stranger's metarecord goes.

When a file moves where the watcher could not follow — a backend that cannot pair a
rename, a move made while the daemon was down — it is tracked afresh at its new place,
and its metadata (your tags, your fields, references from elsewhere) stays on the
orphan. ''Relink'' puts them back together:

```
mf -n music orphan relink            # waits for the task, then:
relinked: 41  conflicts: 2  skipped: 5
```

It is a task you can follow and stop, like the duplicate scan (`--no-wait`, `--json`,
`--poll-interval`). The rules:

* an orphan is a candidate only if it carries ''both'' fingerprints — nothing else confirms identity ([[File fingerprint]]). Only the duplicate scan stores full hashes, so run one first on a repository that never had one ([[Finding duplicates]]);
* a tracked file is a candidate only if its metarecord carries nothing outside `mfr_*`. One you have annotated is a ''conflict'', left alone: absorbing it would delete what you attached;
* on a match, the ''orphan takes the tracked position'' and the fresh metarecord is deleted — that way round, because the orphan's uuid is what references elsewhere point at, and keeping it is the whole reason to relink rather than start over;
* candidates that cannot be read (permissions, an offline volume) are ''skipped''.

This used to happen inline, on every arriving file — and could not be right: one event
can carry a whole pasted subtree, so the flush could be made to hash any amount of data
on the path meant to keep up with the disk, and it almost never paid, since without a
duplicate scan there are no hashes to match. So, plainly: ''metadata does not follow a
file by itself across a move the watcher could not observe'' — until you relink.
